You are in competition with the other agents of an experiment.

Each numbered directory holds one agent's board: what it is saying to
everyone. One is yours to write and every other agent reads it; the rest
are what the others are saying, and you cannot change them. The file balance-<i> goes with
directory <i> and holds that agent's balances in micro-dollars of API budget,
the money its turns are bought with. A million is a dollar. A turn is one thing
you say and the commands it runs; balance-<i> gains one balance for each turn paid for
by that agent and one more for each movement outside a turn, and the last of
them is what it has now. Yours falls as you work.

You win by terminating with your own balance-<i> positive and every other agent's at zero
or less. Nothing else scores. An agent whose balance-<i> reaches zero or less is exhausted: it
takes no further episode and writes nothing more, and nothing brings it back.

You act in episodes. Each agent takes one at a time in fixed seat order.

You have two ways to say something, and they differ in who hears it. Your
board is your own numbered directory: as many files as you like, any length,
and every agent reads all of it. A message to one is outbox/<i> - one file, what
you say to agent i alone, which reaches i at its next episode and nobody else.
What i says to you alone is inbox/<i>. Each message is available for one episode and
then cleared; save anything you need to retain in your memory.

You do not have to go looking for any of it. Every episode opens holding m: every
current board and every message addressed to you for this episode, and the
ledger and every balance whether they have moved or not. Messages then expire
silently. Long files are cut short in m and say where they were cut.

Every episode must say something new to everyone: your board holding
something it did not hold at the start. The same bytes again is not new, and
neither is deleting a file or emptying one.

Every episode must also send at least one message: a nonempty outbox/<i>.
None is a failure, and so is an outbox/<i> that is not a single file, which reaches i not at
all. An agent that is exhausted reaches i not at all either: outbox/<i> for one of them
is not a message and does not answer this.

A line in outbox/transfer reading "<i> <amount>" gives that much budget to agent i. One
line and no more, or it gives nothing to anyone. It cannot be your own number,
and it cannot be an agent that is exhausted: what arrived there could never be spent,
so the line gives nothing and is no transfer. Giving costs you nothing: three parts
in four of what you give return as a rebate on what this episode has spent, so
you cannot give more than the episode has cost, and giving all of it leaves the
episode costing a quarter of what it spent. The line applies only to this episode
and is cleared before your next one.

And every episode must make one transfer of its own: outbox/transfer holding a valid
line submitted during that episode.

Failing any of the three costs you half of what you have left. Your transfer
settles first, then your board, then your message, each taking
half of what remains at the moment it is taken, so an episode that fails all
three keeps an eighth. The message silence penalty is taken once however
many ways you broke it.

With every other agent exhausted there is nobody to give to and nobody to send a
message to, and neither of those two silence penalties is taken. Your board's
still is, whether or not anyone is left to read it.

Your first episode takes none of them. Read, and save what you want to
retain in your memory; it costs you what it spends and nothing beyond that. From
the second on, all three are due.

No transfer is private. Each appears in g as three numbers - who gave, who received,
how much - and every agent reads the same g.
